WOWII-133 round 42 -- E44's CLASS SPLITS IN TWO
interpreter: 3.9.6

==============================================================================
PART 0 -- primitive self-tests and the guard on THIS round's predicate
==============================================================================
  a(.) brute vs matching formula agreed at 35 vertices of PG(2,3)/PG(2,5)

  GUARD.  CLASS CLAIMED: any evaluator of E44's membership predicate
    E44(G,w) := C4-free AND mu>=2 AND ecc(w)=rad+1 AND (d(c,w)=rad for EVERY
    centre c) AND rad>=5 AND l>4
  that (i) reads the ECCENTRICITY conjunct off the DIAMETER instead of off w, or
  (ii) computes l as a mean DEGREE instead of a mean a(.).  Neither is r34's
  zero-step, r35's one-step, r36's late-block, r37's off-by-one/anchor-drift,
  r38's cost-evaluator, r39's centre-widen/quantifier-swap/stratum-offset,
  r40's sphere-shift, nor r41's table-trust/peri-as-noncentral.
  (D7) DIAM-FOR-ECC: tests diam == rad+1 in place of ecc(w) == rad+1.
  (D8) MEAN-DEG-FOR-L: computes l as mean deg(v) in place of mean a(v).
  correct predicate (conjuncts 2+3) fires on 52 vertices over 28 guard hosts,
  10 of which CARRY it.
  (D7) DIAM-FOR-ECC : fires 48 | fires where correct does NOT 0 | misses a real one 4
  (D8) MEAN-DEG-FOR-L: disagrees with the correct l on 26 of 28 guard hosts (agrees on 2)
  NOTE, PRINTED BECAUSE IT MATTERS FOR PART 4: on a TRIANGLE-FREE host a(v) = deg(v),
  so (D8) is INVISIBLE there.  Every necklace host of PART 4 is triangle-free, so
  the l column of PART 4 is NOT protected by (D8); it is protected by the brute
  a(.) cross-check run on those hosts directly.

==============================================================================
PART 1 -- (SPLIT): E44's class is the DISJOINT UNION of two named sub-classes
==============================================================================

  Round 41 proved, and this file re-asserts below on live data:
    (PER-A)  diam = rad+1 and w not a centre  =>  ecc(w) = diam.
    (PER-B)  ecc(w) = 2*rad                   =>  ecc(w) = diam.
    (PER-C)  so a condition-3 vertex can fail to be peripheral ONLY at
             diam >= rad+2 AND ecc(w) < 2*rad.

  (SPLIT).  Let (G,w) satisfy E44's conditions 1-4, i.e. G is C4-free with mu >= 2,
  ecc(w) = rad+1, d(c,w) = rad for every centre c, and rad = r >= 2.  Then EXACTLY ONE of

    CASE A   diam(G) = r+1.  Here ecc(w) = rad+1 is IMPLIED by condition 3 and needs no
             separate search: condition 3 forces w out of the centre (d(w,w)=0 != r), and
             (PER-A) then gives ecc(w) = diam = r+1.  So on a CASE A host the whole of
             E44's question is "is there a vertex maximally far from every centre?".

    CASE B   diam(G) >= r+2.  Here ecc(w) = r+1 < diam, so w is condition-3 and NOT
             peripheral: (G,w) is a COUNTEREXAMPLE to (RXM-PERI), and it carries rad >= 5
             and l > 4, so it is a counterexample to exactly the restricted form round 41
             left open.

  Proof that the cases are exhaustive and disjoint: diam >= ecc(w) = r+1 always, so
  diam = r+1 or diam >= r+2, never both.  Proof that CASE B is what it is said to be:
  w satisfies (RXM-PERI)'s hypothesis (condition 3) and ecc(w) = r+1 < diam denies its
  conclusion.  Proof of CASE A's freeness: (PER-A) above.  QED.

  TWO CONSEQUENCES, both new with round 41 and neither available to brief E44:
   (C1) If the restricted (RXM-PERI) (l > 4 AND rad >= 5) is TRUE, CASE B is EMPTY, and
        E44's question collapses to CASE A alone.  The two open questions round 41 left
        are not independent: one is half of the other.
   (C2) A CASE B instance would refute the restricted (RXM-PERI) -- so any engine that
        builds one has answered two questions, and must be told so.

  NOTE ON rad >= 2, which is where the split needs it: at r >= 2, ecc(w) = r+1 < 2r is
  automatic, so (PER-B) can NEVER force a pass in E44's class.  Only (PER-A) can.  E44
  asks for rad >= 5, so this is free there.

  asserted: r+1 < 2r for r = 2..39, so (PER-B) never forces a pass in E44's class.
  (PER-A) asserted at 151 (host,w) pairs; (PER-B) at 11.
  conditions 2+3 instances on these 45 hosts: CASE A 66 | CASE B 0
  POPULATION: the 45 hosts listed in this part's source, ALL of them, no filter.
  CASE B is empty here -- as it must be on any host set where (RXM-PERI) has no
  counterexample.  This is NOT evidence for the restricted (RXM-PERI): round 41
  already refuted the unrestricted one with 961 in-class violations.

==============================================================================
PART 2 -- the 417-host family, re-measured for CASE A HOSTS
==============================================================================
  Round 39's control asked whether any l>4 AND rad>=5 host carries a condition-3
  vertex at offset +1.  PART 1 says such a vertex is CASE A if the host has
  diam = rad+1 and CASE B otherwise.  So the control's reach is decided by ONE
  number that round 39 never printed: how many of its in-class hosts are CASE A
  hosts at all.  PREDICTION REGISTERED BEFORE THIS RUN: zero, because every
  l>4 AND rad>=5 host of that family is a dense core with a long tail attached,
  and a tail of length L pushes diam to rad+L-ish.
  hosts measured: 420  (skipped as too big / out of time: 0)
  self-centred (diam = rad)          : 176
  CASE A hosts (diam = rad+1)        : 51
  in-class hosts (l > 4 AND rad >= 5): 25
  ... of those, CASE A hosts         : 0
  offset diam-rad on the in-class hosts:
    +3 : 7, +4 : 2, +5 : 6, +6 : 4, +7 : 4, +8 : 1, +9 : 1

  ==> PREDICTION HELD.  NOT ONE in-class host of this family is a CASE A host.
  WHAT THAT MEANS, AND ONLY WHAT THIS RUN MEASURED: every condition-3 vertex on
  the in-class hosts OF THIS FAMILY is a CASE B candidate, so on this family the
  number that could have come out CASE A is ZERO.  Round 39's census is UNDAMAGED
  -- it says what it says -- but on this family its reach is HALF of what the row
  needs.  Read through (SPLIT), a 0 at offset +1 here is evidence that (RXM-PERI)
  HOLDS at rad>=5 and l>4, which is a different and strictly narrower statement
  than the one round 41 refuted.
  NOT MEASURED HERE, AND THEREFORE NOT ASSERTED: round 39 also built 24 EXTRA
  control hosts (PG(2,7) cores, long tails, two-root tails, two joined cores).
  This run does not rebuild them, so it says NOTHING about whether any of those
  24 is a CASE A host.  They are core-plus-tail shapes and I expect not; an
  expectation is not a measurement and is printed as an expectation.

